   "name": "Dry methods of cooking"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Dry methods cook food by dry heat or in hot fat.\n• Baking: in an oven (bread, cakes, meat pies).\n• Roasting: in an oven or over an open fire (roasted plantain, corn, groundnuts, fish).\n• Grilling: under or over direct strong heat (soya/suya, fish, chicken).\n• Toasting: browning bread or seeds.\n• Frying: in hot fat or oil. Shallow frying uses a little oil (eggs, plantain); deep frying covers the food in oil (puff-puff, chips, fish).\nAdvantages: quick (grilling, frying); food is tasty, crisp and brown; it looks attractive; grilling lets fat drip away.\nDisadvantages: fried food absorbs fat (risk of obesity and heart disease); food can burn or dry out; hot oil can cause burns and fires; some methods need a lot of fuel (baking).",
  "checks": []
 },
